#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133595 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "89a4773c-f6bb-577d-8743-5d0a46c1da6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5573" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/36/3693f5fe916109a80a0d7782f7d21781953a7f854cedf82568d353d7e9d23681.svg)\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=1[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133596 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "4985be13-9b44-5ec5-b342-00354339b902" :question/math-academy-id "e-5574" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/16/16f0578d54931024f488302f40489449cc5d8a76bed2dea13b85edd29c486dd2.svg)\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3-2)}^{2}+{(2-2)}^{2}+{(1-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133657 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "82a0e170-d916-5ae7-bd72-9b47c7287e0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7173" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w},$ where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and\n![](images/dd/dd8614491da535daffac48a2966a3f832f81a94f16f4dc1fea8a2bea7d24b61b.svg) \nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane given that $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by   \n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\,\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{13\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{13\\cdot 5+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133659 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "7014f0ec-9ce3-598a-8ddb-b71a000d383f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7309" :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf v,\\mathbf w\\}$ where\n![](images/52/52f781dc28ffc1d3af7a1c381f78fcc3982f195a9e8af406551a93f1c4f5823b.svg)\nFind the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.$ So, computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{10})}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{20}\\,\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{10\\,\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left( \\dfrac{{\\sqrt2}}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b17d38e2-ea85-5107-b604-497b63ec5e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388676} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6cbb82-c062-5a82-8fdc-2ed3dbcdd01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388677} {:answer/id #uuid "3a994a4c-9b54-57bd-b761-85b0c0c8f841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388678} {:answer/id #uuid "85e31f1b-329e-5835-98ed-f620d644671b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388679} {:answer/id #uuid "24cecd39-8b24-5553-898e-7aed91b983ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e31f1b-329e-5835-98ed-f620d644671b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388679} :answer-field/id #uuid "557a82b7-a6fa-592d-905c-d5ecda965a86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "844e86f7-1f0f-5f3f-bcdf-abc293b831b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-140359" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=3[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{4\\cdot (-2)+14\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}w=2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9a3cfb-52c0-5be4-87c1-f76f5ae7b31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269512} {:answer/id #uuid "61497d65-2033-5bdc-986b-b42a650cecc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270127} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8461ba-1dae-5b54-80f1-6dbb83774e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270521} {:answer/id #uuid "90b95c34-7216-5cb0-bccd-fe697695e561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270526} {:answer/id #uuid "f5128844-b902-598e-8586-b0484fd37e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d9a3cfb-52c0-5be4-87c1-f76f5ae7b31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269512} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb0c30a1-420f-58af-8caa-9fbfa56e943e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9852acca-5b7f-59fd-8378-ef1bf83dc23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140367" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{6\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(6-4)}^{2}+{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(-4-(-2))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af016e3c-6583-5076-8547-2da29a030814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481620} {:answer/id #uuid "b6516927-d038-5c53-9ff0-064cf2766c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481624} {:answer/id #uuid "b82ec417-e27b-545f-8fda-47066fad2867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481625} {:answer/id #uuid "b835f025-1455-54be-a739-0b1bfc31db5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481626} {:answer/id #uuid "f65d1b05-4896-5427-ac5b-938cfa80e269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b82ec417-e27b-545f-8fda-47066fad2867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481625} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e97383b-2c44-5d66-99ae-4035a54846c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22b768f5-ca87-5dc0-9a1f-4fdb656c1be5" :question/math-academy-id "q-39990" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{8}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f4b214-6534-5b98-b4e5-57781988dedb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131600} {:answer/id #uuid "a51e9ac4-74c1-52b2-b5a1-d00817115f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131601} {:answer/id #uuid "158d7892-6ff6-548e-895b-cd455c43d195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131602} {:answer/id #uuid "79f15387-e1c4-5043-8fd1-12a999eb87f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131603} {:answer/id #uuid "7e76f06d-0641-5070-8eff-9c08bacf0e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f4b214-6534-5b98-b4e5-57781988dedb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131600} :answer-field/id #uuid "19fd96a4-4435-5e40-b2f9-87a0cc805ff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a716cc5-8a45-5c7c-b271-c73c5ea3248b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40187" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d97ca78-347a-502a-821b-6597b74d8dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131654} {:answer/id #uuid "a47409bb-80da-5d21-bdd0-a7a9eb56e892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131655} {:answer/id #uuid "32f83252-c914-58ec-9f1a-010c8c11df29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131656} {:answer/id #uuid "46373f4f-36ae-5bcb-8d56-e944ad9e67b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131657} {:answer/id #uuid "5a016800-771f-5899-bf47-fcbbd10697e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46373f4f-36ae-5bcb-8d56-e944ad9e67b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131657} :answer-field/id #uuid "399e5cd9-589e-5b64-85f8-862324894700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e4b7092-101b-57be-87cd-a4a4c83a4d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-40215" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{7}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{7}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-\\frac{10}{3})}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{7}{3})}^{2}+{(2-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c04b137-6afd-5c93-bffd-c2ad52b0f5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481581} {:answer/id #uuid "14660518-3268-5b10-8494-338fa52ec7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58.52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481587} {:answer/id #uuid "33a073ec-2091-5bcd-b60b-ebba7bccb98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25.58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481595} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd33462-6550-5cd3-834f-318520fe8bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55.82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481601} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e3caac-58c5-5c9f-b488-f943b767b28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{52.58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14660518-3268-5b10-8494-338fa52ec7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58.52}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481587} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9aaa6a-a4ef-548d-866f-aa031c7198d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "887e0ea0-dd4d-5fcd-a5bc-286204a862b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40217" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11})≈{58.52}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6d61a2-0e5b-5db8-8114-ea09bd548880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324202} {:answer/id #uuid "2876536a-c600-53ac-b3c3-d18398b95b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324295} {:answer/id #uuid "b46e84eb-2d3c-58ae-8d7c-30ba77856452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325522} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dfcad5-b516-516d-800a-2a96e9641759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326167} {:answer/id #uuid "fb36b747-fb26-5f7c-857c-f41f73cd77a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9dfcad5-b516-516d-800a-2a96e9641759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326167} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c99ae4-547c-5385-8356-bd2126cc4e55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57f12766-f0fb-5f9d-b65a-691edb22d947" :question/math-academy-id "q-40218" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+9\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+9\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500c3e97-8bf1-5baa-8484-123e1c2bc43f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324627} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f75d8e-4c78-59b2-bf56-198fcd6fb356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325648} {:answer/id #uuid "d9678edc-a470-57e1-b4c9-c86420a8ea5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325863} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9c4cfa-239a-53ca-9664-a0fef2a7acb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326077} {:answer/id #uuid "f6be27e6-04c8-5cc4-8355-1aa1a320f491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "500c3e97-8bf1-5baa-8484-123e1c2bc43f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324627} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c5c4657-0967-5db0-8a8f-3ee73c469a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "532e9715-de59-568c-a5cc-48ba89edb532" :question/math-academy-id "q-40591" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The vector $x$ can be written as $x={a}_{1}-{a}_{2}.$ So, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, the projection of $x$ onto $S$ is $x$ itself and the angle between $x$ and the subspace is equal to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96be9367-3974-5e43-9065-674c96f9d52e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373039} {:answer/id #uuid "43cb05da-18c7-523d-91ac-2ba4f915beee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373040} {:answer/id #uuid "35fff824-80db-5d3d-a944-43b3f274b775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373041} {:answer/id #uuid "f72442e8-cc3d-5b0d-8121-a36f5f6f77c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373042} {:answer/id #uuid "a143909a-1c57-5867-ab51-079e67a44a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a143909a-1c57-5867-ab51-079e67a44a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373043} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a5bcdf-7363-5a46-9be0-a09ed9a92fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2c213b52-b119-57bd-9ee2-819532e6eca1" :question/math-academy-id "q-40604" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\},$ where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{v\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{w\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{2}{3}{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{18}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3895d9a2-5b55-56b8-8e77-b7594721615f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324430} {:answer/id #uuid "81aebd0f-0f75-5d09-91ef-329b76a12330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325059} {:answer/id #uuid "8f774b5c-323b-5488-b3cb-6252ff983d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325190} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0229ae-0ab3-507b-bf26-462fa715e6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325433} {:answer/id #uuid "b9790be9-fb80-5af9-a377-9a86a7e2ffed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f774b5c-323b-5488-b3cb-6252ff983d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325190} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fe35266-9ed1-52c1-b4b9-a5b45818d6e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59702bd2-b8d6-5ac1-8934-5b2903177011" :question/math-academy-id "q-40615" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The fact that we got the zero-vector as the projection means that $x$ is orthogonal to the subspace $S.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16cd1aca-dd85-5e1f-82c8-641a1346120d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481589} {:answer/id #uuid "6d27de48-288e-5ed9-9d7d-0d0eae6d2993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481607} {:answer/id #uuid "82d0e36d-d943-5853-8c22-a5f342e793d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481613} {:answer/id #uuid "c405d893-f4de-5b73-b692-4d9ba63fe661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481630} {:answer/id #uuid "e241ba1f-eb80-5d8a-bbe0-22c86cdf6403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16cd1aca-dd85-5e1f-82c8-641a1346120d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481589} :answer-field/id #uuid "1538404b-ed05-55f2-a776-3683d1df3c57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae3c05b7-f23f-59a0-b648-be66bb1e3e3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40617" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d2d0b6e-9b33-5b70-809d-a505e2ff9a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481599} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4a973a-58b0-5903-bddb-9f88ac25c8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481604} {:answer/id #uuid "91ba5ea9-cb35-564c-8528-b13463295348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481614} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b3a4d2-a564-5688-9d33-b1279a57f1d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481636} {:answer/id #uuid "fba0c9ac-4d4c-5112-acf7-decbe76a6bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91ba5ea9-cb35-564c-8528-b13463295348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481614} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1fe577e-eb1d-5087-b60c-da2397f3533b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a43e3223-e818-53e0-8311-b6df64e15405" :question/math-academy-id "q-40618" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(0)(0)+(-1)(-1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since we got the zero-vector as the projection, the vector $x$ must be  orthogonal to the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ Thus, the vector $x$ extends the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cae73a3-842e-5a1a-8774-35bba342845c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07028645-efe7-5074-be80-058ffbade705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481579} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb48d44-3d17-50ac-a866-63b0e50f60cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481593} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8768fa-dee4-512e-9646-4b4d01ba6296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481602} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2a905a-0b20-5d17-a56b-1c89e206592e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481608} {:answer/id #uuid "f5069584-b109-5eca-b530-25aa937cda0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5069584-b109-5eca-b530-25aa937cda0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481647} :answer-field/id #uuid "53237724-b18f-554e-b4b7-e3f73a8381db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eff4218e-d396-5a2a-a2a2-2a662de74575" :question/math-academy-id "q-81473" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{4\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-4)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3c1f60c-42a8-5cc3-b99d-7ed826dbf850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394202} {:answer/id #uuid "9474eb4f-22ca-55b6-8e41-9c047ef24322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394203} {:answer/id #uuid "74601550-cf11-537d-9f31-1c0372cb38f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394204} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b582d9-2788-5790-9fb9-5f3bdc69a080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394205} {:answer/id #uuid "70aee89b-0f86-54b7-94b5-9f83e4f0c615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74601550-cf11-537d-9f31-1c0372cb38f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394204} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4389597-0c5f-5733-adbd-f42e7bc02def" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7a96c11-4c5f-5a12-a6e5-6ac0294535f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81580" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-8)\\cdot 4+14\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}v=-2[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-8)\\cdot 1+14\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\text{ }\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008be6d7-980e-5b49-a388-3e4f48435f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348459} {:answer/id #uuid "521e1964-6992-5ed1-ae2f-6c97c17fc564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349194} {:answer/id #uuid "58c19296-aba5-544a-8e2e-8dae30dcc3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349262} {:answer/id #uuid "bf021816-9923-5554-b6cf-06024a0a333a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350142} {:answer/id #uuid "d2350521-6a12-5e8b-a441-760965fb9b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2350521-6a12-5e8b-a441-760965fb9b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350292} :answer-field/id #uuid "140726d8-396d-5c70-ae89-78bc01edc808" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e35039b-7bcf-5005-b2b8-d003962fb201" :question/math-academy-id "q-81583" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cd5d909-284c-501b-b2c9-da1c869a1f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373280} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b780fe-daa1-5ab5-ac25-a80454719e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373281} {:answer/id #uuid "58b544be-9a03-5801-8738-892de0de2bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373282} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c962ad-68a8-5c4a-a710-820e389850eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373283} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d6c9ed-45af-519c-a7a4-8c6729ca5068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5c962ad-68a8-5c4a-a710-820e389850eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373283} :answer-field/id #uuid "5446889c-4194-56bc-877f-e62dc48f37d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f29dd616-20ae-5ac6-8d0f-2176557e28bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81585" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-2)+(-16)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-3)+(-16)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-3)}{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205b1754-05a1-59b8-93b0-db0ef3962104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348725} {:answer/id #uuid "70cb3a55-f980-5061-9a92-c367650f204e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349484} {:answer/id #uuid "771cc871-e2e0-58e4-a814-420aa33e0550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349546} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb1d160-c986-578c-860d-c9b3d57aeb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350375} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7cfda1-2b74-5d6d-9dd9-e6af615d713d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb1d160-c986-578c-860d-c9b3d57aeb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350375} :answer-field/id #uuid "83254343-abdc-5c6a-aae6-d4bfc0083d04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "061b2c31-4a10-5d46-822a-c45bc18a019d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81601" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 3+(-11)\\cdot (-4)+17\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 5+(-11)\\cdot 4+17\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6feecd4-8822-5f34-9d43-bd14c718bbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373320} {:answer/id #uuid "17148ef3-6acc-5564-b61d-01018de840fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373321} {:answer/id #uuid "4153ac73-6a9e-55d0-85c9-e7e7af03259b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373322} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b0c395-4679-520f-9797-456665f24d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373323} {:answer/id #uuid "94ce7d37-7650-556c-afec-2c465c9a5502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17148ef3-6acc-5564-b61d-01018de840fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373321} :answer-field/id #uuid "14b44c30-bfb7-5726-84f7-243f272a60e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d3957bc-82e8-5ac2-b7a9-4a6d13bcdf98" :question/math-academy-id "q-82913" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-4)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161688a0-a709-5d07-bc9b-90b1366c3e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7.18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348629} {:answer/id #uuid "377eaa42-8c8a-5e18-9868-47c039dc8b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48.59}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348947} {:answer/id #uuid "68c76bc1-8c31-5712-9d87-feb476f57eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349410} {:answer/id #uuid "76eacc52-990b-5321-b0cf-a3aa240a1742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29.91}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349542} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0c2240-ec75-53b5-9f99-ed6c5abd200a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{36.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68c76bc1-8c31-5712-9d87-feb476f57eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28.13}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349410} :answer-field/id #uuid "88fe00de-5ec1-50d2-aa0b-e419400fd37b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d244080-c289-59c7-a494-e5da1783db9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82978" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+7\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{\\sqrt{63}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})≈{28.13}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31df133b-9ac7-5b6c-8dfb-2ea8b930102f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348890} {:answer/id #uuid "719d31fd-259c-55ac-8640-902983d5b269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349495} {:answer/id #uuid "a307cfd3-9686-508a-86e7-e55cf358f154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349902} {:answer/id #uuid "a3484bae-8027-5986-9331-75bf9f4f0e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349905} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba27a2-255f-5ae5-bf58-536727e4f685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "719d31fd-259c-55ac-8640-902983d5b269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349495} :answer-field/id #uuid "4de0475a-e24c-5264-b704-1570b396f25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5be09e3c-1b36-5bfe-b41d-3d8f88facd1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82979" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}]}
